\ `see` round-trips a scope.
\
\ The signature is stored as source text in a side table on the word,
\ keyed by the bytecode offset of its FF_OP_SCOPE_ENTER, so it prints
\ back exactly as written. Named inputs compile to indexed reads below
\ the barrier and carry no name at run time, so `see` resolves each one
\ from the signature of its enclosing scope — including from inside a
\ control structure, which pushes its own frames in between.
\
\ This is the other half of the bargain: a signature is only worth
\ trusting if the tool that reads the code can show it.

: max2 { ( a b -- m ) a b > if a else b then } ;
see max2
